explanatory variable distinguishes the tutoring conditions being compared, while the response is the quantitative outcome measured after participation. Age or school may matter, but neither is the stated explanatory variable."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.2","skill":"2.A","q":"A survey records each student's grade level, number of hours slept last night, preferred school-lunch entrée, and backpack mass in kilograms. Which variables are quantitative?","correct":"Hours slept and backpack mass","distractors":["Grade level and hours slept","Preferred entrée category and measured backpack mass in kilograms","Grade level and preferred entrée"],"e":"Hours slept and backpack mass have meaningful numerical magnitudes for which arithmetic comparisons are sensible. Grade level is an ordered label here, and preferred entrée is categorical."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.2","skill":"4.A","q":"A hospital data set codes discharge destination as 1 = home, 2 = rehabilitation, and 3 = skilled nursing. A student reports that the mean destination code is 1.8. What is the main statistical problem?","correct":"The numerical codes represent categories, so their mean has no meaningful interpretation.","distractors":["The mean should be replaced by a z-score because the codes are standardized.","The destination variable is quantitative because its categories are numbered.","The data cannot be summarized because categorical variables have no useful summaries."],"e":"Assigning numbers to categories does not make the variable quantitative. Counts, proportions, or a bar chart are meaningful, but arithmetic on the arbitrary labels is not."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.3","skill":"3.B","q":"In a sample of 240 commuters, 96 report using public transit as their primary mode. What is the sample proportion who use public transit?","correct":"0.40","distractors":["0.60","96/100 = 0.96","240/96 = 2.50"],"e":"The sample proportion is the count in the category divided by the total sample size: 96/240 = 0.40. The complement, 0.60, is the proportion using other modes."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.3","skill":"4.A","q":"A frequency table for 500 households lists 85 with no vehicle, 210 with one vehicle, 160 with two vehicles, and 45 with three or more. Which statement is supported?","correct":"The modal category is one vehicle, containing 42% of the households.","distractors":["The median number of vehicles is exactly one because the largest category is one vehicle.","Most households have at least two vehicles because 160 + 45 exceeds 210.","The mean number of vehicles is 1.33 because the last category can be treated as exactly three."],"e":"The largest count is 210, and 210/500 = 0.42, so one vehicle is the modal category. The open-ended final category prevents an exact mean, and the other numerical claims do not follow."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.4","skill":"3.A","q":"A school surveys students about one of four preferred after-school activities. Which display is most appropriate for showing the relative frequencies of the four categories?","correct":"A bar chart with one bar for each activity","distractors":["A histogram with touching bars","A scatterplot of activity versus frequency","A normal probability plot"],"e":"Preferred activity is categorical. A bar chart appropriately displays category frequencies or relative frequencies; histograms are for quantitative variables and scatterplots require two quantitative variables."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.4","skill":"4.A","q":"Two bar charts show the same categorical counts, but one vertical axis begins at 90 instead of 0. What is the most important consequence?","correct":"The truncated axis can visually exaggerate differences among category counts.","distractors":["The category proportions themselves change when the axis is truncated.","The chart becomes a histogram because the baseline is above zero.","The counts can no longer be recovered from labeled bar heights."],"e":"Changing the displayed baseline does not change the data, but it can make modest absolute differences appear much larger. Statistical interpretation should distinguish visual emphasis from the underlying counts."},{"unit":"U1","topicCode":"1.5","skill":"3.A","q":"A teacher wants a display that preserves the individual values of 22 quiz scores while also showing the shape of their distribution. Which display is best suited?","correct":"A dotplot","distractors":["A pie chart","A segmented bar chart","A mosaic plot"],"e":"A dotplot place